      "sequence_position": 16,
      "problem": "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(1)}=(-7)(0.3)^{2}=(-7)(0.09)=-0.63,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(2)}=(-7)(0.3)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-7)(0.3)^{4}}{(-7)(0.3)^{2}}=(0.3)^{2}=0.09.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.63)}{1-0.09} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0.63}{0.91} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
